  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does steam push a piston?",
      "a": "Water turning to steam expands enormously, about 1,700 times. Let into a closed cylinder, the steam presses on the piston and pushes it along; then the steam is let out or condensed so the piston can return.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a steam engine make things move?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why was Watt's separate condenser so important?",
      "a": "It let the cylinder stay hot all the time while the steam was cooled elsewhere, so far less fuel was wasted reheating metal. Watt's engines used roughly a quarter of the coal of a Newcomen engine for the same work.",
      "asks": [
        "What did James Watt improve?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What did the beam do in a steam engine?",
      "a": "The beam was a huge rocking lever on a pivot. One end was linked to the piston and the other to a pump or crank, carrying the force across.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a beam engine?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a steam engine governor do?",
      "a": "It is a pair of heavy balls spun by the engine. If the engine speeds up, they fly outwards and close the steam valve a little; if it slows, they drop and open it, keeping the speed steady.",
      "asks": [
        "How did steam engines control their speed?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How efficient were early steam engines?",
      "a": "Very inefficient. Newcomen engines turned only about 1 percent of the coal's energy into work, and Watt's best engines a few percent; modern steam turbines reach over 40 percent.",
      "asks": [
        "Were steam engines efficient?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "James Watt patented his separate condenser in 1769.",
    "The unit of power, the watt, is named after James Watt."
  ],
